Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||09-13-2001||10:33 AM||||My dad has told me its a good idea to keep the clutch in gear when parked.  I got in the car and I played with the shifter, I noticed that when the engine was off, I could slip out from fourth and into first, and the clutch wasn't even in.  Is this normal?<P>I will look into the search history but when I cold started the car it stays at 2,000 RPMs.  Also revving the engine it would stay where it was and then go down slowly to 2,000.  Yesterday while I was driving the idle speed is 800 or 900 but when I was playing with the clutch and accelerator, it does happen when I disengage the clutch and if the engine was revved it would stay at 2,000 and hover there.  When I approached intersections or anywhere that I have to slow down, I press the clutch in and slow down, I noticed my RPMS doesn't go down, would stay at 4000 or 3000.  Would climb down really slowly or when I put the engine back in gear.  My friend says maybe the EGR or something.  (He will come and look at it and help me fix it anytime soon)  Is it supposed to ALWAYS go down to 900 every time I have the clutch fully disengaged?<P>------------------<BR>1984 2m4<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||09-13-2001||10:58 AM||||Yes, you can shift among the gears if the engine is off and the car sitting.  Since nothing is moving there is no grinding.  You should use the park brake or it will seize up and leave the car in a low gear when parked.<P>A high idle may indicate a vacuum leak or a bad sensor.  The rubber lines get hot and cold and eventually may crack.  If you listen with a piece of garden hose as a stethoscope, you may hear a hiss at the leak.<P>If you depress the clutch while driving and hold it, yes it should return to normal idle speed.<P>Gary<p>[This message has been edited by FieroBUZZ (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
